{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The light of the righteous rejoices, but the lamp of the wicked is extinguished (Proverbs 13:9).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b>, king, peace be upon him, likened the soul of the righteous to light because the soul is an intellectual light received from the Throne of Glory; and that light is from His essence and being itself — it does not come to it from any other place, nor is it dependent upon any other thing. And he likened the soul of the wicked to a lamp, for the light of a lamp is not from itself but depends on another thing, namely the oil and the wick: as soon as the oil and the wick are consumed, the lamp is immediately extinguished. So too the soul of the wicked is dependent upon the existence of its body, for the essence of its joy is only in the body's joy; therefore it is consumed when the body is consumed — and the 'consuming' is that it does not shine in the light of life. And even though the soul has no death, it is punished with a punishment more bitter than death; and because it did not strive in the ways of light but in the ways of darkness, therefore the attribute of darkness comes upon it. And this is the utter perishing of the soul, and it is the punishment that has no end and the torments that have no limit; and this is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (ibid. v. 11): ״במות אדם רשע תאבד תקוה״ — 'When a man, a wicked one, dies, hope perishes.' He did not say 'when a wicked man dies' but 'when a man, a wicked one, dies,' to make clear that one who follows after the offspring of the earth (adamah) and immerses himself in bodily desires is called wicked; therefore, upon his death, his hope perishes with him, for his hope was only in the affairs of his body, not in the affairs of his soul; and for this reason, when his body perishes, his hope perishes. And from this he said ידעך — 'is extinguished' — which is the nullification of light, and it is the punishment of the wicked soul. And it appears proper to say that the expression of דעיכה (dimming) is not complete nullification, but rather that it does not shine and does not attain the radiance proper to it; thus Bildad said (Job 18:5): ״גם אור רשעים ידעך ולא יגיה שביב אשו״ — 'Indeed, the light of the wicked dims, and the flame of his fire does not gleam'; whereas the expression כבוי (quenching) is complete nullification, as the verse witnesses (Isaiah 43:17): ״דעכו כפשתה כבו״ — 'they were extinguished (daḵhu) like flax, they were quenched (kavu).' But regarding the excellence of the righteous soul he said ישמח — 'rejoices' — and that is the reward, for the expression שמחה means an increase of attainment and the spirit of prophecy (ru'aḥ ha-kodesh), as it says (Psalms 104:34): ״אנכי אשמח בה'״, and likewise (ibid.): ״ישמח ה' במעשיו״. And because the righteous serve the Holy One, blessed be He, with joy, therefore they attain the excellence of joy in the World to Come, for the attribute of joy comes upon them and draws their souls and sustains them forever. And there is yet another reason why he likened the righteous soul to light: because it was created on the first day together with the creation of the primordial light. And there is a verse that speaks of the souls of the righteous that testifies to this, namely what is written (I Chronicles 4:23): ״עם המלך במלאכתו ישבו שם״ — 'with the King in His work they dwelt there'; and it is destined for that light created on the first day. We find five lights in the parashah, and likewise the soul has five names. That light was the beginning of all created beings, and concerning it David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 119:130): ״פתח דבריך יאיר מבין פתיים״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'פתח דבריך יאיר — from the very opening of Your mouth there was light,' as it says (Genesis 1:3): ״ויאמר אלהים יהי אור ויהי אור״. That light the Holy One, blessed be He, brought forth from within the darkness — not like a king of flesh and blood: a king of flesh and blood kindles a lamp from a lamp, but can one kindle a lamp from within darkness? But the Holy One, blessed be He, brought forth light from within the darkness, as it is written (ibid. v. 2): ״וחשך על פני תהום״, and immediately after it: ״יהי אור ויהי אור״. And thus Scripture says (Job 12:22): ״מגלה עמוקות מני חשך ויוצא לאור צלמות״, and likewise Daniel said (Daniel 2:22): ״הוא גלא עמיקתא ומסתרתא ידע מה בחשוכא ונהורא עמיה שרא״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: עמיקתא — this is the Account of the Chariot (ma'aseh merkavah); ומסתרתא — this is the Account of Genesis (ma'aseh bereshit); ידע מה בחשוכא — 'He knows what is in the darkness' — ״וחשך על פני תהום״; ונהורא עמיה שרא — 'and light dwells with Him' — ״ויאמר אלהים יהי אור״. Since the Holy One, blessed be He, brought forth light from within the darkness, He has no need for the light of flesh and blood; thus said David the king, peace be upon him (Psalms 139:12): ״גם חושך לא יחשיך ממך ולילה כיום יאיר כחשיכה כאורה״. And since all is equal before Him, blessed be He — ״כחשכה כאורה״ — what is this that is written (ibid. v. 11): ״ישת חשך סתרו״? This is an expression for the abundance of concealment regarding the apprehension of the Supreme Being, for He likened His apprehension to darkness because in a dark place it is impossible to grasp what is within it. And Scripture alludes to you that our matter is the screen that prevents and the darkness that separates between HaShem, may He be exalted, and ourselves, for we have no power to see Him; and this is, in my opinion, what is written (Song of Songs 2:9): ״הנה זה עומד אחר כתלנו״ — 'Behold, He stands behind our wall' — meaning that this אלי, 'my God,' and אנוהו — and a wall separates between us and Him, and it is matter; and understand this. If so, סתרו חשך is from the perspective of our apprehension, which is limited by our being in matter, whereas all is light from the perspective of the essence of His glory; and this is what it says: ״כחשיכה כאורה״. And even though all is light and He has no need for our light, we were commanded in the Tabernacle and in the Temple, for the honor of the House upon which the Shekhinah rests, to kindle the lamps of the Menorah — and this is what is written." "<b>When you kindle the lamps, toward the face of the Menorah shall the seven lamps give light.</b> The expression בהעלותך is on account of the flame that rises of itself. And the Sages, of blessed memory, make it an expression of elevation, for thus they said in the Midrash: 'בהעלותך — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: It is not that I need your light, but rather that you give light before Me in the manner that I illuminated for you in the wilderness with the pillar of fire, in order to elevate you before the nations — that they may say: How Israel gives light before the Holy One, blessed be He, since He gives light to all! For from within darkness I brought forth light, and I have no need for your light. Now, behold, the eyes that are in a person — within them there is white, yet one does not see through the white but through the black. And furthermore, this lightning (beraq) is a product of the supernal fire, and it goes forth and flashes to the entire world, as it says (Ezekiel 1:13): ״ודמות החיות מראיהן כגחלי אש בוערות כמראה הלפידים״… ״ומן האש יוצא ברק״ — and it flashes to the entire world, and I have no need for your light. And why did I command you? In order to elevate you — בשביל להעלותך — to elevate you before the nations.' Until here. And Aaron was commanded in this parashah regarding the kindling of the Menorah, for the hour and for the generations: ״דבר אל אהרן״ — for the hour, since the Menorah was entrusted to him and the obligation of kindling was upon him; ״ואמרת אליו״ — for the generations. And since the commandment applies also to the generations, you will not find in this parashah that it mentions באהל מועד as it says in another place (Exodus 27 [referring to 27:21]): ״באהל מועד יערוך אותו אהרן ובניו״ — lest you say that in the Tent of Meeting they needed this light because there were no windows there, but in the Temple (bet ha-mikdash), where there were broad windows, sealed (חלוני שקופים אטומים), they would not need it; therefore the verse left it unspecified." "<b>And know</b> that the windows that were in the Temple (bet ha-mikdash) were narrow on the inside and broad on the outside, and this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: The way of the world, when a person builds a house, he makes windows narrow on the outside and broad on the inside, so that the light enters from outside and illuminates within. But Solomon did not do so; he built the Temple and made there windows narrow on the inside and broad on the outside, so that the light would go forth from the Temple and illuminate outward — to make known that it is all light and needs no illumination. And this is what is written (I Kings 6:4): ״ולבית עשה חלוני שקופים אטומים״ — 'And for the house he made windows, broad (shaqufim), sealed (atumim).' That is, shaqufim — open toward the outside; atumim — sealed toward the inside, i.e., narrow. And from this Scripture says in Ezekiel (40 [referring to 40:16]): ״וחלונים לו ולאלמיו סביב סביב כהחלונות האלה״ — it did not say כחלונות but כהחלונות, with the addition of the letter heh, to allude that the windows of the Temple were in the shape of the letter heh, which is narrow on the inside and broad on the outside, so that the light would go forth from there to the outside. And our Rabbis of blessed memory further interpreted: כהחלונות — it is not written כחלונות but כהחלונות, an expression of כהה (dim); that is, dim windows that were narrow on the inside and broad on the outside, toward the outer eastern side. And therefore the Temple is called Ariel (Isaiah 29 [referring to 29:1]), from the word אריה (lion): just as a lion is narrow from behind and broad from in front, so too the windows of the Temple. And this parashah teaches three things: the first, that the faces of the lamps should be directed toward the central one, namely the western lamp; the second, that the Menorah should preferably be of gold — but if one made it of other kinds of metal, it is valid, provided one neither adds nor subtracts in the number of lamps, for there must be seven, no fewer and no more; and the third, that it be מקשה (made of a single piece), that is, all one unity from a single ingot. Therefore it repeated and wrote ״מקשה היא״, to make clear that מקשה is indispensable, but gold is not indispensable." "<b>Toward the face of the Menorah shall the seven lamps give light.</b> If you examine the language of Scripture carefully, you will find that it should have said: יאירו ששת הנרות — 'the six lamps shall give light' — since מול פני המנורה, 'toward the face of the Menorah,' is the central lamp, and all six were directed toward it." "<b>And by way of the plain sense (peshat):</b> the measure of the verse is: 'When you mount the lamps — toward the face of the Menorah shall you mount them' — and together with it they shall be seven, and this is 'the seven lamps shall give light'; and therefore it was not possible to say 'the six lamps shall give light,' since they are seven lamps that give light." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> the verse is established and clear according to its plain sense, and it speaks of the Menorah above. And the meaning of 'toward' (אל מול) — it says: opposite the Menorah above, which are seven Sefirot in Wisdom (בחכמה); 'the seven lamps shall give light' — the lamps below. And this, in my opinion, is the secret of the language in which it expressed the kindling with the language of ascending (עליה): 'בהעלותך את הנרות'; and it says 'העלה נרותיה'; and it says (Exodus 30:8) 'ובהעלות אהרן את הנרות'; (ibid. 40) 'ויעל הנרות לפני ה', and this is what you find in the Menorah of Zechariah (Zechariah 4:2): 'ושבעה נרותיה עליה שבעה,' whose meaning on this path is: 'and her seven lamps' — upon it, opposite the seven above; and afterward, 'ושבעה מוצקות לנרות אשר על ראשה.'" "<b>Also</b> in this passage you will find the mention of the Menorah four times. The first two, which speak of the Menorah above, are plene (מלאים), and they are: 'אל מול פני המנורה יאירו,' 'אל מול פני המנורה העלה.' And the last two are deficient (חסרים), which speak of the Menorah below, and they are: 'וזה מעשה המנורה מקשה זהב' — this is the Menorah below, and therefore it mentioned gold; and it says 'עד ירכה עד פרחה,' to explain that it speaks of a physical Menorah — and it is deficient, missing the vav: 'כן עשה את המנרה,' deficient in vav. And the form המנורה (plene) is not found in all the Torah except three times: two in this passage, and the third (Exodus 25:31) 'תיעשה המנורה.' And so it is found in the Masorah: three plene in the Torah, and your mnemonic is 'תיעשה יאירו העלה.' And this is what it explained: 'כמראה אשר הראה ה' את משה' — above; 'כן עשה את המנרה' — below." "<b>The seven lamps.</b> The matter of the Menorah is very hidden and wondrous; I have already hinted at it in the section ויקחו לי תרומה, and there you will understand, through the central lamp, a wondrous sign (פלאי אל) for Israel." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'The seven lamps' — to what may the matter be compared? To a king who had a beloved friend, and the king said to him: 'Know that I am dining at your place; prepare a place for me.' He went and prepared according to his ability. When the king came, he came with attendants and secretaries on this side and that, and golden Menorahs on this side and that. When the beloved friend saw all this glory, he was ashamed and hid everything he had prepared for him. The king said to him: 'Did I not tell you that I am dining at your place? Why did you not prepare anything for me?' He said to him: 'My lord the king, I saw all this glory that came with you, and I was ashamed, and I hid everything I had prepared.' The king said to him: 'By your life, I am setting aside all the vessels I brought with me, and I will use only yours, for love of you.' So too the Holy One, blessed be He — He is entirely light, yet He commands Israel to make a Menorah before Him and to kindle it continually. When Moses made the Tabernacle and the Menorah, and the Shekhinah came — what is written there? (Exodus 40) 'And Moses was not able to enter the Tent of Meeting.' Immediately, (Leviticus 1) 'And He called to Moses'; and 'when Moses came into the Tent of Meeting to speak with Him, he heard the Voice speaking to him' — and what is the matter? 'בהעלותך את הנרות.' Israel said: The Holy One, blessed be He, created the sun and the moon that illuminate the entire world — does He then desire that we kindle lamps before Him? When the Temple was destroyed, the Menorah was hidden away because of the power of its holiness, as the Sages of blessed memory interpreted: Moses was perplexed by its crafting, and the Place commanded him to cast a talent of gold into the fire, and it came out fashioned on its own, as it says (Exodus 25) 'תיעשה המנורה' — on its own; and it is written (ibid.) 'מקשה' — that is, how hard (קשה) was its making. <br><b>And this</b> is the work of the Menorah. The height of the Menorah was eighteen handbreadths, corresponding to the numerical value of וז\"ה (18). 'מקשה' teaches that Moses was perplexed (נתקשה). And so the Sages of blessed memory interpreted: in four things Moses was perplexed, until the Holy One, blessed be He, had to show him with a finger, and they are: the Menorah, as it says 'וזה מעשה המנורה'; the offerings, as it says (Leviticus 6) 'זה קרבן אהרן'; the shekels, as it says (Exodus 30) 'זה יתנו'; the new moon, as it says (ibid. 12) 'החדש הזה לכם.' And this is the meaning of מקש\"ה: מ'נורה ק'רבן ש'קלים ה'חדש. And even though the Sages of blessed memory said: the Holy One, blessed be He, grabbed Moses and said to him, 'This you may eat and this you may not eat,' and concerning the creeping things, where it is written (Leviticus 11) 'וזה לכם הטמא' — they did not say he was perplexed except in four things."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {2 "Citation reads שמות כ״ו; the verse quoted (באהל מועד יערוך אותו אהרן ובניו) is Exodus 27:21, not Exodus 26. Translated the citation as written; the discrepancy appears to be authorial or scribal.", 3 "Citation reads ישעיה כ״ט for the name Ariel applied to the Temple; the reference is to Isaiah 29:1. Translated as written.", 7 "In the phrase 'עד ירכה עד פרחה' he cites Numbers 8:4 without explicit chapter:verse; these words appear there. The mnemonic 'תיעשה יאירו העלה' compiles the three plene occurrences of המנורה by their distinctive surrounding words.", 9 "The mnemonic מקש\"ה is an acrostic of מנורה, קרבן, שקלים, החדש; the letter-values are not gematria but notarikon. The number 18 for וז\"ה = 6+7+5 is exact as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:40.462426869Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}